.TH CAI 1 "" "cai" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cai \- terminal coding agent
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cai
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cai
starts the Smith coding agent in the current directory or the directory selected
with
.BR \-C .
It uses ChatGPT authentication from
.I ~/.codex/auth.json
first, then credentials in cai's lockd store if the Codex file is absent, and starts with the
.B gpt-6-luna
model at medium reasoning effort.
The prompt is
.BR "\(dq> \(dq" .
Provider-issued reasoning summary headings appear in the message bar; their
remaining body and assistant text stream through the terminal renderer in
prompt mode. Non-interactive activity is logged through
libpslog; only the final assistant response is rendered on standard output.
.PP
The newest saved session for the workspace resumes automatically.
Sessions are scoped to the canonical workspace directory.
Use
.B \-N
to start a new session.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-state-dir " DIR, " \-\-cache-dir " DIR, " \-\-data-dir " DIR"
Override individual application roots. Defaults use the matching XDG home and
the application name (cai for this binary). Environment aliases are CAI_STATE_DIR,
CAI_CACHE_DIR and CAI_DATA_DIR; INI keys are in [paths].
.TP
.BR \-\-history-dir " DIR, " \-\-clipboard-dir " DIR, " \-\-log-dir " DIR"
Override prompt history, pasted images, or interactive logs independently.
Defaults follow state_dir/history, cache_dir/clipboard, and cache_dir respectively.
.TP
.BI \-\-pouch-key-file " FILE"
Override the encryption-key filename (default state_dir/pouch.key). Sharing an
encrypted Pouch root requires sharing the same key.
.TP
.BI \-\-namespace-prefix " NAME"
Default storage namespace prefix (cai). Individual --sessions-namespace,
--scopes-namespace, --auth-namespace, --todo-namespace and --messages-namespace
flags override complete namespaces; matching keys are in [storage].
.TP
.BI \-\-queue-prefix " NAME"
Prefix for automatic mailbox and receipt queue names (default cai). A single
lowercase identifier, at most 32 bytes. --message-queue overrides the complete
public mailbox name instead. Also [messaging] queue_prefix or CAI_QUEUE_PREFIX.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
Load another INI configuration file, creating a documented, commented template
if it is missing. Defaults to $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cai/config.ini, or
~/.config/cai/config.ini. Existing files are never rewritten.
.TP
.B \-\-no-config
Skip configuration loading and template creation. Incompatible with --config.
.TP
.B \-\-chrome
Enable Chrome browser tools (Smith default) through the installed ChatGPT
extension and CAI's native messaging host. Connection is lazy; an ordinary turn
needs no connected browser. Does not install or change a host registration.
.TP
.B \-\-no-chrome
Disable Chrome tools. Also configurable with CAI_CHROME or [chrome] enabled.
.TP
.BR \-\-web-search " / " \-\-no-web-search
Enable/disable hosted web_search on Responses providers (default on).
Chat Completions has no hosted search; SearXNG is available separately.
.TP
.BR \-\-todo " / " \-\-no-todo
Enable/disable todo_kanban (default off). CAI uses a shared encrypted cai.todo
document, with streamed transactional updates, rather than a todo.json file.
.TP
.BI \-\-todo-layout " board|list"
Kanban display layout (default board). Board columns contain bold IDs above
italic titles capped at 28 Unicode characters, including an ellipsis when
truncated. List retains full titles. Also CAI_TODO_LAYOUT or [ui] todo_layout.
.TP
.BI \-\-board " NAME"
Initial kanban board (CAI_BOARD or [todo] default_board; default "default").
The flag selects or creates a board and overrides the resumed selection once.
It does not enable the optional todo tool. Each conversation saves its selection.
.TP
.BR \-\-messaging " / " \-\-no-messaging
Enable/disable shared-Pouch conversation messaging (default off). Each active
workspace listens on an allocated name in namespace cai.messages using managed
consumer services. Names start with cai.<basename>; collisions add the fewest
distinguishing real ancestor components, skipping shared directories. The actual
hostname is a leading component for distinguishing hosts. Character length is
not an optimization objective. Registered names stay stable across restarts and
new conversations. Automatic naming uses the host machine-id and canonical path
as workspace identity; duplicate hostnames with identical paths on distinct
hosts require an explicit name. Names that cannot fit the backend's lowercase
ASCII alphabet and 128-byte limit unchanged also require an explicit name.
The agent_message tool lists named mailboxes or sends text by recipient queue
name. Delivery ACK means durable acceptance, not user
approval or model completion. Isolated review does not enable messaging.
.TP
.BI \-\-message-queue " NAME"
Override the derived workspace mailbox name. Also CAI_MESSAGE_QUEUE or
[messaging] queue_name. Names are lowercased, allow ASCII letters, digits,
dot, underscore and hyphen, and have at most 128 characters. Empty keeps
automatic naming. /status shows the name. The newest app start claims new
deliveries; already delivered messages stay in their original conversation.
Exiting an older instance cannot release a newer owner's binding.
Unrepresentable or indistinguishable workspace names require an explicit
override. Destination discovery includes offline mailboxes;
only delivery ACK confirms acceptance.
.TP
.BR \-q , " " \-\-queue " " NAME
Destination mailbox for a standalone CLI message. Requires --message.
.TP
.BR \-M , " " \-\-message " " TEXT
Send text to --queue and exit. No provider credentials, model request,
conversation creation or workspace mailbox takeover occurs. Uses the session
storage endpoint without a receipt listener. Writes JSON with status queued,
message_id and recipient to stdout after liblockdc accepts the enqueue.
Errors use pslog on stderr and return nonzero. Does not wait for delivery,
approval or execution; offline recipients are supported. These messages have
no delivery deadline or queue expiry. --message-ack-timeout-ms applies only
to the agent messaging tool.
.TP
.BR \-\-autoqueue " / " \-\-no-autoqueue
Automatically approve incoming TUI messages (default off). Alt-A focuses
approval, Alt-Y approves, Alt-N rejects and Alt-E edits/requeues. Approved
messages start ordinary turns after current work finishes; they do not steer.
Pending messages survive restart. Non-interactive runs acknowledge delivery
but leave incoming prompts pending for an interactive run.
.TP
.BI \-\-message-ack-timeout-ms " MS"
Deadline from sending to delivery ACK, default 300000 (five minutes), range
1..86400000. Unaccepted messages expire; a missing ACK is a tool error.
Configuration keys are [messaging] enabled, enable_auto_enqueue, ack_timeout_ms;
environment variables are CAI_MESSAGING, CAI_ENABLE_AUTO_ENQUEUE and
CAI_MESSAGE_ACK_TIMEOUT_MS.
.TP
.BR \-\-searxng " / " \-\-no-searxng
Enable/disable function-based SearXNG search (default off).
.TP
.BI \-\-searxng-url " URL"
Override [searxng] base_url (default http://127.0.0.1:8888).
.TP
.BR \-\-revgeo " / " \-\-no-revgeo
Enable/disable reverse geocoding (default off).
.TP
.BI \-\-revgeo-url " URL"
Override [revgeo] base_url (default https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org).
.TP
.B \-\-[no-]read-file, \-\-[no-]list-files, \-\-[no-]apply-patch
Enable/disable individual file capabilities (default on).
.TP
.B \-\-[no-]view-image, \-\-[no-]goals, \-\-[no-]skills
Enable/disable image inspection, model goal tools and skill discovery (default
on when the model/catalog permits). These policies also narrow review tools.
.PP
Tool settings use CLI > environment > INI > built-in defaults. Boolean variables
are CAI_WEB_SEARCH, CAI_CHROME, CAI_TODO, CAI_SEARXNG, CAI_REVGEO,
CAI_READ_FILE, CAI_LIST_FILES, CAI_APPLY_PATCH, CAI_VIEW_IMAGE, CAI_GOALS,
CAI_SKILLS, CAI_TERMINAL, CAI_IMAGE_GENERATION, CAI_REVIEW_SUBAGENT and
CAI_CODEX_AGENTS_MD, using true/false values. INI keys live under [agent]
(lowercase names without CAI_) except [chrome] enabled. The existing [env]
precedence rule is applied before resolving settings.
CAI_SEARXNG_URL, CAI_REVGEO_URL and CAI_CHROME_SOCKET select service endpoints.
The default template documents tool-specific timeouts and limits. Isolated
reviews omit hosted search, browser, kanban and optional service tools.
.TP
.BI \-\-chrome-socket " PATH"
Select a connected CAI Chrome host socket; also enables browser tools.
Discovery otherwise requires exactly one live host in the private runtime
or cache directory.
.TP
.B \-\-chrome-setup
Register this executable as Chrome's com.openai.codexextension native host
and exit. Never overwrites an existing foreign or unrecognized registration.
No browser is launched and no provider authentication is required.
.TP
.B \-\-chrome-remove
Remove only a registration verified to belong to this executable and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-chrome-manifest " FILE"
Override the setup/removal manifest path, for another native-host location.
The file must be named com.openai.codexextension.json.
.TP
.BR \-N , " " \-\-new
Start a new session in the workspace.
.TP
.BR \-n , " " \-\-non-interactive
Run supplied instructions or a goal to completion and exit without reading
from the terminal. Requires at least one
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-g ,
or a review mode.
.TP
.BI \-\-resume " [ID]"
With no ID, list numbered conversations for the current directory and exit.
With an ID, resume that saved session in the current directory.
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-new .
.TP
.BR \-C , " " \-\-directory " " DIR
Change to
.I DIR
before opening auth, sessions, or the agent. Relative paths are resolved
from that directory. The default is the current directory.
.TP
.BR \-i , " " \-\-instruction " " TEXT
Submit
.I TEXT
as a prompt after saved conversation replay. Repeat to queue ordered turns;
each prompt starts after the previous turn or goal ends.
.TP
.BR \-g , " " \-\-goal " " TEXT
Create or replace a durable goal with objective
.IR TEXT .
The agent continues until the goal finishes or stops. In interactive mode,
the prompt then remains available; in non-interactive mode cai exits.
.TP
.B \-\-review
Run one isolated review against the Git default branch and exit. Pass the
argument
.B uncommitted
to select staged, unstaged, and untracked changes instead. The default is
resolved locally from origin/HEAD, or a single unambiguous main, master, or
trunk branch. Missing or ambiguous defaults require an explicit scope.
With
.BR \-\-base ,
review changes against a reference; with one
.BR \-i ,
use custom review instructions. Review activity and reasoning go to standard
error as pslog records. Validated findings go to standard output unless
.B \-\-out
is specified.
.TP
.B \-\-review-and-fix
Start a new, non-interactive Smith session with a durable goal to invoke the
built-in review subagent, fix actionable findings, verify, and repeat until
the review is clean. There is no default iteration limit. Requires the review
subagent. Errors and stopped goals return a nonzero status.
The default scope is the Git default branch; pass
.B uncommitted
to review and fix only uncommitted changes.
.TP
.BI \-\-base " REF"
Review against
.I REF
in either review mode.
.TP
.BR \-o , " " \-\-out " " FILE
Write only the final review findings to
.IR FILE .
Standard output remains empty; review activity remains on standard error.
Requires
.BR \-\-review .
.TP
.BR \-T , " " \-\-output-type " " TYPE
Select
.B markdown
(default) or
.B json
for final review findings. Requires
.BR \-\-review .
.TP
.BR \-a , " " \-\-auth-json " " PATH
Import ChatGPT authentication from
.IR PATH .
The default source is
.IR ~/.codex/auth.json .
Otherwise use credentials in the lockd store. Unchanged source files preserve
refreshed stored tokens. Token refresh never changes the source file.
.TP
.B \-\-login
Open the browser login flow, save ChatGPT auth in the cai.auth namespace of
cai's lockd store, and exit. Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-auth-json .
.TP
.BR \-l , " " \-\-list
List all conversations across directories and exit. Rows show the ID,
directory, and first prompt, truncated to terminal width or 80 columns.
.TP
.BI \-\-lockd " ENDPOINT"
Select an absolute pouch:///path/to/root or remote http:// or https:// lockd
endpoint. Default: the Pouch root under cai's XDG state directory.
Selected Pouch roots use cai's key, durable sync, indexing, and shared writer
settings. Remote encryption and access policy are managed by the server.
.TP
.BI \-\-lockd-client-pem " FILE"
Enable remote mTLS with a combined client certificate, private key, and CA
PEM bundle. HTTPS verifies server certificates.
.TP
.BI \-\-export " ID"
Export resumable JSONL and Markdown for a conversation and exit. Markdown uses
libcai's existing exporter, including instructions and runtime/goal metadata.
No model requests or tools run during export. Files are plaintext, mode 0600.
.TP
.BI \-\-export-dir " DIR"
Override the export root. Default: $XDG_DATA_HOME/cai/exports, falling back to
~/.local/share/cai/exports.
Each conversation writes ID/ID.jsonl and ID/ID.md beneath this root.
.TP
.BI \-\-import " FILE"
Validate a JSONL export, assign a new ID in the current directory, and exit.
Failed imports are not published in conversation lists.
.TP
.B \-\-no-shell-context
Keep future !COMMAND results out of model context.
Terminal handoffs (!sh and /shell) always remain local and are never recorded.
The positive --shell-context enables sharing. CAI_SHELL_CONTEXT and
[ui] shell_context supply startup defaults. Commands still execute with
normal user permissions, display literal output and save only the newest
sanitized tail that fits the replay parser limit.
Earlier shared results remain in context.
.TP
.BI \-\-shell-context-max-lines " N"
Retain the last N sanitized logical lines of shell output (default 5000).
Overrides CAI_SHELL_CONTEXT_MAX_LINES and [runtime] shell_context_max_lines.
.TP
.BI \-\-shell-context-max-bytes " N"
Bound retained sanitized UTF-8 bytes (default 4194304, minimum 4).
Overrides CAI_SHELL_CONTEXT_MAX_BYTES and [runtime] shell_context_max_bytes.
Old complete lines are discarded first; oversized lines retain a bounded suffix.
.TP
.BR \-p , " " \-\-provider " " NAME
Select
.BR chatgpt ,
.BR openai ,
.BR openrouter ,
.BR berget ,
or
.BR custom .
The default is
.BR chatgpt .
OpenAI reads
.BR OPENAI_API_KEY ;
OpenRouter reads
.BR OPENROUTER_API_KEY ;
Berget reads
.BR BERGET_API_KEY .
Berget uses the Chat Completions API and resumes sessions using local history.
Its default model supports text, image URLs, and function tools, including
local and MCP tools. Hosted image generation is disabled; file IDs and
arbitrary file attachments return an error.
.TP
.BI \-\-endpoint " URL"
Base URL for the
.B custom
provider. Required with
.BR custom .
.TP
.BI \-\-api-key-env " NAME"
Environment variable containing the custom provider API key. The default is
.BR CAI_API_KEY .
.TP
.BI \-\-model-metadata " FILE"
Load provider model metadata fallback JSON from FILE. By default, cai reads
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cai/models.json (or ~/.config/cai/models.json) if it exists.
Live provider fields take precedence; the fallback fills omitted fields.
For Berget, libcai also carries model-card context limits when the API omits
them. A provider value wins over the file, which wins over the compiled limit.
Berget prices are estimated in EUR and are never converted to USD.
.TP
.BR \-m , " " \-\-model " " ID
Select the model.
ChatGPT and OpenAI default to
.BR gpt-6-luna ;
OpenRouter defaults to
.BR openai/gpt-5.6-luna ;
Berget defaults to
.BR zai-org/GLM-5.3-Flash .
The custom provider requires an explicit model.
.TP
.BR \-r , " " \-\-reasoning-effort " " LEVEL
Set reasoning effort:
.BR none ,
.BR minimal ,
.BR low ,
.BR medium ,
.BR high ,
.BR xhigh ,
.BR max ,
or
.BR ultra .
The default is
.BR medium .
.TP
.BR \-\-reasoning-tool " / " \-\-no-reasoning-tool
Enable the model's set_reasoning tool (default off). It accepts only normal
and high modes and is instructed to raise effort unless explicitly told to
return to normal. It never automatically lowers a higher current effort when
requesting high. Changes apply at the next durable tool boundary and the
selected actual effort is restored on conversation resume.
.TP
.BI \-\-normal-reasoning-effort " LEVEL"
Normal tool mapping, default medium, independent of -r. Also
CAI_NORMAL_REASONING_EFFORT or [agent] normal_reasoning_effort.
.TP
.BI \-\-high-reasoning-effort " LEVEL"
High tool mapping, default high. Also CAI_HIGH_REASONING_EFFORT or
[agent] high_reasoning_effort. CAI_REASONING_TOOL and [agent] reasoning_tool
control whether the tool is enabled. Unsupported or unadvertised choices
warn the user and return a successful no-op to the model. The task continues;
the model is not asked to diagnose CAI. Configure valid mappings and model
metadata when the provider does not advertise its choices.
.TP
.BI \-\-service-tier " NAME"
Select provider routing, for example default (explicit normal), priority
(Fast), or flex. With no selection, a saved tier resumes; new sessions leave
routing unset. Environment CAI_SERVICE_TIER and [agent] service_tier
configure the same setting. Account/model availability is provider-controlled.
.TP
.BI \-\-reasoning-summary " MODE"
Set provider reasoning summary mode:
.BR none ,
.BR auto ,
.BR concise ,
or
.BR detailed .
The default is
.BR concise .
.TP
.BI \-\-review-model " ID"
Select the model for review children.
.TP
.BI \-\-review-reasoning-effort " LEVEL"
Set reasoning effort for review children.
.TP
.BI \-\-review-reasoning-summary " MODE"
Set reasoning summary mode for review children.
.TP
.BI \-\-agents-md " PATH"
Use the global cai AGENTS.md file at
.I PATH
instead of the default under cai's XDG config directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-config-dir " DIR"
Use
.I DIR
as the global agent configuration directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-skills-dir " DIR"
Use
.I DIR
as the global skills directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-install-skill " DIR"
Validate and install the directory's SKILL.md plus nested artifacts, then exit.
Defaults to $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cai/skills or ~/.config/cai/skills; --config-dir
selects DIR/skills and --skills-dir overrides that root. The package is named
from YAML metadata. Preserves executable files and rejects links/devices/FIFOs.
No authentication, model requests or artifact execution occurs.
.TP
.B \-\-replace
With --install-skill, explicitly replace an existing skill package. Publication
is atomic; copy/validation failure preserves the previous package. Without this
flag, an existing package is an error.
.TP
.BI \-\-identity " TEXT"
Set the visible agent identity.
.TP
.BR \-I , " " \-\-developer-instructions " " TEXT
Append developer instructions.
.TP
.B \-\-codex-agents-md
Load
.I ${CODEX_HOME:-$HOME/.codex}/AGENTS.md
after cai's global AGENTS.md, then discover ancestor AGENTS.md files from the
repository root to the workspace.
Each loaded source is identified as current user-provided AGENTS.md instructions
and working context, including any stated user role or preferences. Complete
file contents and loading order are preserved when resuming a conversation.
.TP
.B \-\-no-image-generation
Disable hosted image generation.
.TP
.B \-\-no-terminal
Disable terminal tools.
.TP
.B \-\-no-review-subagent
Disable the built-in reviewer.
.TP
.BI \-\-tool-output " MODE"
Select interactive tool previews: compact (default) or full.
Compact previews show five command output lines and one surrounding patch
context row, with a vertical ellipsis between separated changes.
Full previews retain supplied command and MCP output and all patch context.
Tool output limits still apply. Non-interactive activity remains complete
pslog output on stderr, independent of this preview setting.
.TP
.BR \-v , " " \-\-verbose
Print runtime events; repeat for event sequence numbers.
.TP
.BR \-h , " " \-\-help
Show command-line help.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH PROMPT COMMANDS
.TP
.BR "/review" " [uncommitted|base REF|commit SHA|TEXT]"
Run an isolated reviewer between turns and render Markdown findings. With no
argument, compare against the Git default branch. Explicit uncommitted, base,
commit, or custom text selects that scope. Findings become context in the
current conversation. Escape or Ctrl-C cancels; normal prompts entered during
the review queue for the parent conversation.
.TP
.BR "/review-and-fix" " [uncommitted|base REF]"
Replace the current goal with the review, fix, and verification loop in this
conversation. With no argument, compare against the Git default branch.
There is no default iteration limit. Requires goal tools and the review subagent;
finish any isolated review or shell first. The TUI stays open when pursuit
completes or stops. Use /goal pause or /goal clear to control pursuit.
Unlike the startup flag, this command does not create a new session or select
non-interactive mode.
.TP
.BR "/todo" " [list|use NAME|new NAME|layout board|list|help]"
Show the selected kanban board, list shared boards, select an existing board,
or create/select a board. Changes require a stable boundary between turns.
The default board layout groups cards into TODO, DOING and DONE columns.
/todo layout list selects the full-title item list; /todo layout board restores
the board. /todo layout reports the current setting. Layout changes apply only
to future output in this invocation, including across /new and /resume, without
changing stored cards or past output. Layout changes are allowed during turns.
The selected board is included in /status and restored with the conversation.
.TP
.B /help
Render help as Markdown tables of slash commands and prompt keys, with startup
examples. This command is local and does not send a model request.
.TP
.B /resume
List saved sessions for the current workspace, newest first.
.TP
.BI /resume " NUMBER"
Load the numbered session from that list and replay saved user prompts and
assistant text without repeating tool side effects.
.TP
.BI /export " [ID]"
Export the active session, or the supplied ID, as JSONL and Markdown.
.TP
.B /new
Start a new session in the current workspace and show its ID and workspace
as a Markdown summary after creation succeeds. Include the active message queue
when messaging is enabled.
.TP
.BI /autoqueue " [on|off]"
Toggle automatic approval of incoming conversation messages. Explicit on
also approves waiting messages; off leaves existing approvals intact.
The setting affects this live TUI and does not rewrite config.ini.
.TP
.BI /queue " [on|off|NAME]"
Show the current message queue, enable or disable messaging and its tool with
on or off, or override its full name. Changes apply between turns and retain
the active conversation and saved model settings without replaying its text.
Approve or reject queued incoming prompts before disabling, preserving edits.
Deliveries still in storage remain available when enabling again.
Automatic approval is controlled separately
by /autoqueue and survives these changes and /new.
No prefix or hash is appended to an explicit name; names are
lowercased. The override lasts for this app, including /new and /resume,
without changing config.ini or session settings. A new owner takes over new
deliveries; previously delivered messages and approvals stay in their original
conversation. The old destination has no listener from this app after renaming.
.TP
.BI /model " [MODEL [EFFORT]]"
Show current and pending settings and the provider catalog, or select MODEL
and optional reasoning EFFORT in the same conversation. A lone known effort
(such as /model high) changes only effort. A model without an effort keeps
the current effort. ChatGPT lists every model returned by the signed-in account
catalog without local filtering, including unfamiliar IDs. OpenRouter skips
catalog listing; other providers show at most 50 models. Explicit model IDs
remain selectable. Idle changes apply immediately;
an active request or tool finishes before selection applies at the next durable
tool-round or turn boundary, before the next model request. Later changes merge
into pending settings. Applied settings are checkpointed together and restored
on resume. Explicit startup model, effort, and reasoning-summary flags override
only their corresponding saved values. New sessions use startup defaults.
Catalog discovery uses a separate client and cache. Explicit model and reasoning
listings refresh discovery using the configured client timeout. Failed refreshes
preserve a previously loaded catalog when available. Repeat the command to retry
immediately.
.TP
.BI /tier " [list|NAME]"
Without arguments, cycle service tiers. Use list to display available request
IDs and current/pending selection, or NAME to select one. ChatGPT uses its
signed-in per-model catalog; OpenAI API request options do not guarantee
account/model access. Changes apply at the next safe model/tool boundary and
survive resume. Explicit unsupported advertised choices fail unchanged.
.TP
.B /fast
Toggle Fast/priority and explicit normal routing. Requires a known Fast choice
to enable it. This is a convenience over /tier; it does not change the model.
.TP
.BI /reasoning-tool " [on|off|normal LEVEL|high LEVEL]"
Inspect or configure the optional reasoning tool between turns. Applies to
this app, including new/resumed conversations; does not modify config.ini.
.TP
.BI /reasoning " [EFFORT]"
Set reasoning effort, or list provider-advertised choices and the default for
the selected or pending model. When model-specific metadata is unavailable,
show the accepted vocabulary with a notice that provider support is unknown.
Reject choices outside a known provider list. Uses the same durable safe
boundaries as /model.
.TP
.BI /shell-context " [on|off]"
Show or change model sharing for future explicit shell commands. Each command
captures the setting at start. Disabling sharing does not disable execution,
erase history or remove earlier model context.
.TP
.BR /trim " [COUNT]"
Without COUNT, list the newest 20 whole conversation entries, newest first.
Row 0 means keep current history. COUNT removes exactly that many newest entries;
0 removes nothing and an excessive count fails unchanged. Use
.B /trim list LIMIT
to change the listing length. Summaries are single lines clipped to terminal
width and rendered through libmdf.
A turn includes its reasoning, steering, tool calls and results. Standalone
recorded shell executions are entries; executions started during a model turn
belong to that turn. Compacted context is one indivisible entry, never expanded
into earlier turns. Boundaries are recovered from the active session's own
journal when needed; no other conversation is inspected or changed.
Requires an idle runtime with no pending work or open shell. The cut is durable
for model context, restart replay and exports; checkpoint failure restores the
original history. External effects, settings, goals and incurred usage remain.
Old storage checkpoints and diagnostic logs are not erased.
.TP
.BR /shell , " " !sh
With no arguments, hand the real terminal to $SHELL -i, falling back to
/bin/sh -i when SHELL is unset or empty. Requires terminal input and output
and an idle agent. These commands cannot be queued or steered; a busy draft
remains editable. The shell uses normal user permissions in the workspace.
CAI restores terminal ownership, prompt state and geometry after exit, even
following a screen clear or resize. A real PTY relays output and foreground
job control. Terminal output is never journaled, logged or shared with the model,
regardless of shell-context settings. Logs record only the executable and exit
metadata.
Launch failures and nonzero exits report details and return to CAI.
.TP
.BR /shellshare , " " !sh+
Open the same interactive shell, explicitly sharing its newest sanitized output
after exit, even when ordinary bang-command sharing is disabled. Older output
falls out of a ring buffer bounded by the configured line, byte and replay
parser limits. Only the newest text that fits is saved and logged;
an unfinished shell's capture is discarded. The saved completion includes exit
status, duration and a truncation notice when needed. Each saved string fits
the replay parser limit, including metadata.
.TP
.BI ! COMMAND
For other bang commands, run $SHELL (or /bin/sh) with -c in the workspace using normal user permissions,
outside the agent sandbox. Stdin is closed; stdout and stderr use one pipe,
without a PTY. Output is literal, without Markdown parsing. Printable UTF-8,
newlines and tabs are preserved; invalid bytes and other controls appear as
\\xNN. Ctrl-C or Escape cancels the shell first, and quit cancels it too.
The timeout is one hour. Background descendants are terminated at completion.
Ordinary prompts entered while it runs are durably queued until completion.
Only the newest sanitized tail is journaled on completion. Resume replays it
without execution. Shared results become tagged user-role context, with exit code and
duration, at a safe model/tool boundary without starting a model turn. At most
5000 logical lines and the configured byte ceiling (default 4 MiB) are retained,
further limited by the replay parser's dynamic-string bound (currently 128 KiB)
including completion metadata and a truncation marker. Oldest text is discarded
as new output arrives. ANSI/VT colors and control strings are
removed before buffering; carriage return, backspace and horizontal editing
update the current logical line. This is not a full screen emulator;
JSONL exports contain only the retained tail. Markdown exports use retained
model context. Slash commands echo as plain $ input rows; normal user prompts
keep their > prefix. The gray ! message line receives ANSI-free libmdf text.
.TP
.BI /goal " [COMMAND]"
Show the current durable goal with status, token accounting, and active time.
The status command also shows this view; help renders the command table.
Prefer asking the agent to create and manage goals.
.TP
.BI /goal " new TEXT"
Start and pursue a fresh, unbudgeted goal, replacing any current goal without
confirmation and resetting accounting. During a turn, replacement applies at
the next safe model/tool boundary. Completion leaves the interactive prompt open.
.TP
.B /goal pause
Pause pursuit after the current request or tool round finishes.
.TP
.B /goal resume
Resume pursuit of a paused, blocked, or limited goal. Existing session usage
limits still apply.
.TP
.B /goal clear
Remove goal tracking without cancelling the current user turn. Repeating clear
is harmless. Goal state is persisted with the conversation. Unknown commands
and missing new objectives are errors, never implicit goal instructions.
.TP
.BI /output " [compact|full]"
Show or change the tool preview mode. The setting applies to future tool calls;
active calls and past output retain their original presentation.
Successful patches show the agent\(aqs submitted patch, with green backgrounds
for additions and red backgrounds for removals across full terminal rows.
The gutter shows original file line numbers for removals and result file line
numbers for additions and context. Wrapped continuations align below the
content and retain the background. Tabs expand to spaces; UTF-8 glyphs wrap
by terminal cell width. Terminals narrower than 32 columns omit the gutter.
Unchanged rows have no background. Activity headings are bold bright cyan, independently of the
bright green/red success/failure star. No file or Git comparison is performed.
NO_COLOR disables colors.
.TP
.B /status
Show a Markdown table of provider, model, reasoning, context, and available
cost or quota metrics. For ChatGPT, fetch quota immediately and update the
status line. Summarize installed local, hosted and MCP tools and server status.
List whether CAI global and Codex global AGENTS.md instructions were loaded,
whether Codex compatibility was disabled, and how many repository instruction
files were captured. These values describe the current developer context,
including after resume; they do not merely check whether a file exists now.
.TP
.B /tools
Show the installed tool inventory, descriptions, input formats and MCP server
ownership, including disabled servers and discovery failures. Available during
a turn; inspecting cached metadata does not trigger more network discovery.
.TP
.BR /quit , " " /exit
Exit the agent.
.SH STATUS LINE
Before the first turn, the thinking line shows
.RB \(lq "Welcome to cai. Send /help for usage." \(rq,
including after replaying a resumed conversation.
The status line shows model and reasoning effort, context used as a percentage,
the workspace, available ChatGPT subscription quota and credits or API cost estimate,
the Git branch when available, and an active goal.
Quota windows are labeled by their returned duration, including weekly-only
accounts and any shorter window returned by the backend. Quota is fetched at
startup and after completed turns in the background, without delaying prompt
readiness. Idle sessions do not poll. Automatic requests are limited to one
attempt every five minutes.
Unavailable values are hidden.
Credits follow the quota windows as
.B crd 43
or
.BR "crd unlimited" ,
when the provider reports them. Balances are rounded to whole numbers here.
Fractional balances appear in
.BR /status .
The message above it shows the first nonblank line of the provider's concise
reasoning summary and the active turn's elapsed time. Libmdf renders its Markdown
as plain text; Softline applies the message theme and accepts no ANSI styling.
Single-line summaries appear only here. Remaining reasoning body streams
through libmdf in the feed, with a
.B "? "
prefix on each rendered row and no separate reasoning label. Softline supplies
its default
.B "! "
prefix to that message. Before a summary arrives it shows
.BR Working .
After the turn settles it shows
.B Worked for
followed by its duration and a local
.I ddhhmm
date-time group (day of month, 24-hour clock, minute). Durations show minutes
and seconds below an hour, hours and minutes below a day, and days, hours, and
minutes thereafter.
.SH LOGGING
All diagnostics and runtime activity use libpslog with the
.B LOG_
environment prefix and JSON output by default. Ordinary prompts, assistant
messages, and reasoning summaries use info level. Conversation records carry
role and prompt_kind fields to distinguish normal prompts, queued turns,
steering, developer instructions, and reasoning summaries. No app field is added.
.PP
In prompt mode, all levels append to the session cache log. Only warning and
error diagnostics are surfaced in the UI as readable notices with explanations
and available request context, including HTTP status, provider code, and
request ID. Warnings, errors, and local command confirmations wrap through
libmdf as literal text. Diagnostic stars remain red, continuation rows align
under the message, and paths and punctuation keep their original spelling.
Notices have one blank row before and after; NO_COLOR disables star colors.
Full pslog records remain in the log file; truncated notices
refer to that file. Startup records are moved into the
selected session log; failed startup logs remain in a startup-* file.
.PP
Provider errors with code cyber_policy or cyber_notice trigger one automatic
explanation-only prompt in the same conversation, using the selected model.
Only enabled workspace-scoped read_file and list_files tools are available,
with at most eight sequential tool rounds. Commands, edits, MCP, hosted tools,
and goal actions are unavailable. The prompt asks for a bug-report summary, possible
triggers, and an assessment of the visible security context, distinguishing
facts from hypotheses. The model cannot determine the provider's internal
trigger or certify a false positive. The prompt and response are recorded
in the conversation and logs. The original error still fails the run;
the interrupted task is not automatically continued. Goal state remains
unchanged. In interactive mode, an explicit next user prompt runs normally
with the configured tools; diagnostic restrictions do not persist into it.
If the explanation fails, that failure is logged without another attempt.
.PP
With
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-\-review ,
or
.BR \-\-review-and-fix ,
activity goes to standard error as pslog records. Only the latest top-level
assistant response of the entire invocation goes to standard output. Replay,
reasoning, tool activity, and child reviews are log-only. If a later turn fails
without a reply, the latest available reply is still emitted. Final response
selection uses a private, unlinked file-backed spool; activity logging remains
immediate. Review findings retain their selected Markdown or JSON format;
.B \-\-out
keeps standard output empty. Utility commands retain their normal stdout results.
.PP
Rendered assistant output detects its stdout destination: terminals allow ANSI
styling, while pipes and files receive escape-free UTF-8 at 80 columns.
.PP
Listing, resume listings, export, import, and login default to warning-level
diagnostics on standard error. Agent sessions and review modes keep all levels
by default. LOG_LEVEL overrides these defaults. Login URLs, callback guidance,
and successful login results are normal standard output.
.PP
Color defaults to automatic terminal detection for stdout/stderr logs.
Redirected output and interactive log files stay uncolored.
Native environment settings include LOG_MODE, LOG_LEVEL, LOG_NO_COLOR,
LOG_FORCE_COLOR, LOG_PALETTE, LOG_VERBOSE_FIELDS, LOG_DISABLE_TIMESTAMP,
LOG_TIME_FORMAT, LOG_UTC, LOG_OUTPUT, and LOG_OUTPUT_FILE_MODE.
LOG_OUTPUT overrides the default sink; default+PATH retains the default sink
and its UI warning notices while adding a file. LOG_LEVEL=warn filters out
ordinary conversation messages.
.SH PROMPT HISTORY
Interactive recall uses $XDG_STATE_HOME/cai/history, falling back to
$HOME/.local/state/cai/history. The canonical workspace path is hashed with
SHA-256 into a .history filename. Recall retains the newest 1000 prompts and
commands for this directory across conversations and restarts. Accepted input
appends immediately, including steering; replay and Ctrl-R selection do not
append. /new preserves recall. Ctrl-R Enter selects a match for editing;
a subsequent Enter submits it.
.PP
Prompt history is stored in private plaintext files, separately from encrypted
conversation and authentication storage. Directories use mode 0700; files and
lock files use 0600. The native store appends without per-entry fsync or an
exit-time save. Persistence failures stop acceptance and report native errors.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.codex/auth.json
Preferred ChatGPT authentication file when present.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/cai/pouch
Encrypted default Pouch root containing cai.sessions and cai.auth namespaces,
plus a derived cai.scopes index for newest-session lookup. Missing or interrupted
directory indexes are rebuilt from session metadata.
Uses ~/.local/state/cai/pouch when XDG_STATE_HOME is unset.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/cai/pouch.key
Plaintext encryption key, mode 0600. Back up this original key with the root;
losing it makes encrypted data unrecoverable. Cai refuses to generate a
replacement for an existing root. Uses ~/.local/state/cai/pouch.key when
XDG_STATE_HOME is unset. The cai state directory must be private (mode 0700).
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/cai/exports/ID/ID.jsonl
Default resumable conversation export, including checkpoint and journal.
Uses ~/.local/share/cai/exports/ID/ID.jsonl when XDG_DATA_HOME is unset, empty,
or relative.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/cai/exports/ID/ID.md
Default Markdown conversation export. Exports are plaintext private files;
the authentication namespace is excluded.
.TP
.I $XDG_CACHE_HOME/cai/SESSIONID.log
Private session activity log (mode 0600). Uses ~/.cache/cai/SESSIONID.log
when XDG_CACHE_HOME is unset.
.TP
.I $XDG_CACHE_HOME/cai/clipboard/XID.png
Ctrl-V X11 clipboard image cache (PNG or JPEG, mode 0600).
Uses ~/.cache/cai/clipboard when XDG_CACHE_HOME is unset. Files remain after
exit so they can be consumed later; remove them when no longer needed.
The view_image tool can read workspace images and this directory, but cannot
read unrelated cache files, follow symlinks outside those roots, or read
hardlinked images.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cai/AGENTS.md
Default cai global instructions when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set; otherwise
.IR ~/.config/cai/AGENTS.md .
.SH CONFIGURATION
The --mcp and --no-mcp flags control configured MCP servers (default enabled).
.PP
Named [mcp.NAME] sections configure Streamable HTTP tool servers. Enabled
servers require endpoint; api_key_env optionally names a Bearer-token
environment variable. enabled defaults to true. discovery_timeout_ms defaults
to 3000 for the complete initialization and paginated tools refresh;
timeout_ms defaults to 30000 for normal calls. Discovery failures warn and
leave other tools usable. Catalogs are cached until restart. Server names use
ASCII letters, digits, underscores and hyphens. Exposed tool names are
mcp_LENGTH_NAME_TOOL, where LENGTH is the byte length of NAME. This keeps
server and tool names distinct even when either contains underscores. The
complete alias must fit 64 bytes, including the prefix.
.PP
--no-mcp, CAI_MCP=false or [agent] mcp=false disables configured servers;
--mcp enables them. Utility commands and isolated --review skip MCP discovery.
The generated template includes a commented [mcp.example] section.
.PP
The generated template documents all supported settings. Explanatory comments
start with ##; commented assignments start with #. Remove one marker to select
the value explicitly. Automatic global instruction, skills and export paths
are shown as resolved at template creation. Leaving them commented retains
automatic lookup; empty values have the field-specific meaning documented
beside each setting.
Uncomment an assignment to select it explicitly. Configuration overrides
built-in defaults; CLI flags override configuration. Saved model and reasoning
settings are restored on resume unless explicitly overridden by startup flags.
Help and version do not create a configuration file. --config-dir DIR selects
DIR/config.ini as well as the global instruction directory.
An explicit --config-dir stays anchored to its original location when a
configured workspace changes the working directory.
.PP
Numeric values use a dot decimal separator regardless of locale.
Paths support ~/ and are relative to the configuration file. In [env], normal
process variables (including empty values) take precedence by default. Set the
global config_env_has_precedence = true to reverse that precedence. Environment
assignments are literal and apply before authentication, logging and workers.
Generated files are private, mode 0600, and never copy live credentials.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns zero after a normal exit, a completed non-interactive goal, or a
successful review. Returns two for invalid command-line arguments or
configuration and one for
startup, runtime, review, or stopped-goal failure.
Provider failures show the available HTTP status, provider code, request ID,
and diagnostic detail. Non-interactive errors are written to standard error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.I README.md
in the installed
.I share/doc/libcai
directory.
.SH CHROME
Install the official ChatGPT Chrome extension, run
.BR cai\ --chrome-setup ,
then open the extension and run
.BR cai\ --chrome .
The registration points to this executable's permanent path. Chrome launches
its native-host mode automatically; no OpenAI executable or Node runtime is
required. The host supports browser control, not OpenAI's Codex side panel.
.PP
The fixed host name may conflict with OpenAI's registration. CAI refuses to
replace it and reports the path. Removal likewise refuses foreign manifests.
Setup also refuses to shadow standard system-wide Chrome or Chromium
registrations.
Setup uses CHROME_CONFIG_HOME, then XDG_CONFIG_HOME, then ~/.config as the
base for google-chrome/NativeMessagingHosts/com.openai.codexextension.json.
.PP
Host sockets live under $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/cai/chrome, or
${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/cai/chrome when XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is unset.
One client may use a host at a time; select a socket if multiple hosts exist.
Browser access is opt-in, is not inherited by subagents, and is rejected by
isolated review runtimes. Tabs are released after each turn; mark deliverable
tabs with the chrome tool's keep action. Replaying history has no browser effects.
.PP
The browser protocol is internal to the extension and may change. Offline
verification does not establish compatibility with a live installed version.
Native-host diagnostics always use stderr; LOG_OUTPUT is ignored in that mode
to protect framed stdout. JSON frames are buffered and bounded; requests
are limited to 1 MiB, and responses default to 16 MiB.
